---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import VerdictBox from '../components/VerdictBox.astro';
import NewsletterForm from '../components/NewsletterForm.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site';
import { breadcrumbs } from '../lib/schema';

const crumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
  { name: 'About', path: '/about/' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`About ${SITE.name}`}
  description="Who we are, why this site exists and how it pays for itself. Software reviews written for people who run a shop rather than a software team."
  path="/about/"
  jsonLd={[breadcrumbs(crumbs)]}
>
  <Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} />

  <div class="wrap pt-8">
    <h1 class="font-condensed text-5xl font-bold uppercase leading-none sm:text-6xl">
      About {SITE.name}
    </h1>

    <VerdictBox class="mt-6" label="The short answer">
      <p>
        We review the software local service businesses run on - scheduling, dispatch, invoicing,
        payments and phones - by buying it, using it on real jobs and timing the tasks that cost
        money. We are funded by partner links, disclosed on every page.
      </p>
    </VerdictBox>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap-narrow prose-shop mt-12">
    <h2>Why does this site exist?</h2>

    <p>
      Because almost every "best field service software" list is a feature table assembled from
      vendor marketing pages by someone who has never dispatched a technician. The lists agree
      with each other because they are copied from the same source, and they are useless for the
      one decision they claim to help with.
    </p>

    <p>
      A shop owner does not need to know whether a product has a feature. They need to know how
      long it takes to raise a quote on a phone in a driveway, whether the app works in a
      basement, and what the bill looks like when they hire a fourth person. Those answers only
      come from using the thing.
    </p>

    <h2>Who is this for?</h2>

    <p>
      Owners of local service businesses between one and about thirty people: plumbers, HVAC
      contractors, electricians, cleaning companies, landscapers, salons, fitness studios and
      independent auto repair shops. Everything here is written for someone who is still close
      enough to the work to know what a bad day looks like.
    </p>

    <p>
      It is not written for enterprise buyers, and we do not cover software priced for them.
    </p>

    <h2>What do we actually do?</h2>

    <p>
      We buy every product at the advertised price, set it up without vendor help, run real jobs
      through it for at least five weeks, and time the same set of tasks on every product. Then we
      try to leave, because how hard a product is to escape is part of what you are buying.
    </p>

    <p>
      The full method, the scoring rubric and the weights are on
      <a href="/how-we-test/">how we test</a>. It is worth reading before you trust a number on
      this site.
    </p>

    <h2>How is it funded?</h2>

    <p>
      Partner links. When a reader books a demo or starts a trial through one of our links we may
      earn a commission at no extra cost to them. No vendor pays for coverage, a score, or a
      ranking, and no vendor sees a review before it publishes.
    </p>

    <p>
      The full arrangement, including what we will and will not do, is on the
      <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">affiliate disclosure</a> page.
    </p>

    <h2>What we get wrong</h2>

    <p>
      Software changes, prices change, and a product that was right in March can be wrong by
      November. That is why every review carries the date it was last fully retested and the date
      the next test is due, and why we would rather cover fewer products properly than more of
      them badly.
    </p>

    <p>
      If something on this site is wrong, tell us and we will re-run the test. Corrections are
      made on the page and dated. <a href="/contact/">Get in touch</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="mt-16">
    <NewsletterForm />
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
